<sn><md>#### 1. Understanding the Four States in Git

Before learning undo commands, understand the basic Git workflow:

```text
Working Directory
       │
       │ git add
       ▼
Staging Area
       │
       │ git commit
       ▼
Local Repository
       │
       │ git push
       ▼
Remote Repository
```

For example:

```text
file.php
   │
   ├── Modified but unstaged
   │
   ├── Staged
   │
   ├── Committed locally
   │
   └── Pushed to GitHub
```

Each state requires a different undo technique.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 2. Check What You Need to Undo

Always start with:

```bash
git status
```

For example:

```text
On branch main

Changes not staged for commit:
  modified: index.php

Changes to be committed:
  modified: config.php

Untracked files:
  test.php
```

This tells you exactly where your changes are.

You can also inspect differences:

```bash
git diff
```

Shows **unstaged changes**.

```bash
git diff --staged
```

Shows **staged changes**.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 3. Undo Local Changes in a File

Suppose you modified:

```text
index.php
```

but have **not committed** the changes.

Check:

```bash
git status
```

If you want to completely discard the changes:

```bash
git restore index.php
```

The file returns to the version from the latest commit.

**Example**

Before:

```text
Hello World
```

You modify it to:

```text
Hello Git
```

Then:

```bash
git restore index.php
```

It becomes:

```text
Hello World
```

**Important**

This permanently discards the uncommitted changes in that file.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 4. Undo All Unstaged Local Changes

If you modified many files and want to discard **all unstaged changes**:

```bash
git restore .
```

This restores all tracked files to their committed state.

For example:

```text
modified: index.php
modified: style.css
modified: script.js
```

Run:

```bash
git restore .
```

All three files return to their last committed versions.

**Warning**

This can permanently destroy your uncommitted work.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 5. Undo a File That Was Staged

Suppose:

```bash
git add index.php
```

Now:

```bash
git status
```

shows:

```text
Changes to be committed:
    modified: index.php
```

You want to **remove it from staging**, but keep the modifications.

Use:

```bash
git restore --staged index.php
```

Now:

```text
Staging Area
     ↓
removed

Working Directory
     ↓
changes remain
```

So:

```bash
git restore --staged index.php
```

does **not** delete your changes.

It only removes the file from the staging area.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 6. Unstage All Files

If you staged several files:

```bash
git add .
```

and want to unstage everything:

```bash
git restore --staged .
```

Your changes remain in the working directory.

For example:

```text
Before:

Working Directory → modified
Staging Area       → modified
```

After:

```bash
git restore --staged .
```

you get:

```text
Working Directory → modified
Staging Area       → clean
```

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 7. Unstage and Discard the Changes

If you want to both:

1. remove a file from staging, and
2. delete its modifications

you can use:

```bash
git restore --staged index.php
git restore index.php
```

Or, depending on your Git version, you can restore both working tree and index from `HEAD`:

```bash
git restore --source=HEAD --staged --worktree index.php
```

This returns the file completely to its latest committed state.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 8. Undo an Untracked File

Suppose you created:

```text
test.php
```

but never ran `git add`.

`git status` shows:

```text
Untracked files:
    test.php
```

Git does not track this file yet.

You can delete it normally:

```bash
rm test.php
```

On Windows CMD:

```cmd
del test.php
```

If you want Git to remove untracked files:

```bash
git clean
```

First preview what would be deleted:

```bash
git clean -n
```

Then actually delete:

```bash
git clean -f
```

For untracked directories too:

```bash
git clean -fd
```

**Very important**

`git clean` can permanently delete untracked files.

Always use:

```bash
git clean -n
```

first.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 9. Undo the Last Commit but Keep the Changes

Suppose you made:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Update UI"
```

Then realize:

&gt; I committed too early. I want to modify the files more.

Use:

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

The commit disappears, but the changes remain **staged**.

```text
Before:

Working Directory
       ↓
Staging Area
       ↓
Commit A
```

After:

```text
Working Directory
       ↓
Staging Area
       ↓
HEAD
```

The changes are still staged.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 10. Undo the Last Commit and Unstage the Changes

Use:

```bash
git reset --mixed HEAD~1
```

or simply:

```bash
git reset HEAD~1
```

This removes the last commit while keeping the file modifications in the working directory.

Afterwards:

```bash
git status
```

will show:

```text
Changes not staged for commit:
    modified: index.php
```

So:

```text
Commit removed
      ↓
Changes kept
      ↓
Changes unstaged
```

This is one of the most useful ways to undo a local commit.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 11. Undo the Last Commit and Delete the Changes

If you want to completely destroy the last commit **and its changes**:

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

This moves `HEAD` backward and makes the working directory match that commit.

```text
Before:

A → B → C
        ↑
       HEAD

After:

A → B
    ↑
   HEAD
```

Commit `C` is removed from the current branch.

&gt; **Warning**
&gt; `--hard` can destroy uncommitted work.

Never use it casually.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 12. Understanding `HEAD~1`

You will frequently see:

```bash
HEAD~1
```

It means:

&gt; The parent of the current commit.

For example:

```text
A → B → C
        ↑
       HEAD
```

Then:

```text
HEAD      = C
HEAD~1    = B
HEAD~2    = A
```

Therefore:

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

means:

&gt; Move the current branch one commit backward.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 13. Undo Multiple Local Commits

Suppose history is:

```text
A → B → C → D → E
              ↑
             HEAD
```

To remove the last three commits:

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~3
```

Result:

```text
A → B
    ↑
   HEAD
```

Again, this rewrites local history.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 14. Undo a Commit but Keep Its Changes

There are three important reset modes:

| Command                    | Commit removed? | Changes kept? | Changes staged? |
| -------------------------- | --------------: | ------------: | --------------: |
| `git reset --soft HEAD~1`  |             Yes |           Yes |             Yes |
| `git reset --mixed HEAD~1` |             Yes |           Yes |              No |
| `git reset --hard HEAD~1`  |             Yes |            No |              No |

This is one of the most important Git concepts.

**Soft**

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

**Undo commit, keep changes staged.**

**Mixed**

```bash
git reset --mixed HEAD~1
```

**Undo commit, keep changes unstaged.**

**Hard**

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

**Undo commit and discard changes.**

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 15. Fix the Last Commit Instead of Undoing It

Sometimes you don't actually need to undo the commit.

Suppose you forgot to include:

```text
style.css
```

in the last commit.

Stage it:

```bash
git add style.css
```

Then modify the previous commit:

```bash
git commit --amend
```

Or:

```bash
git commit --amend --no-edit
```

`--no-edit` keeps the existing commit message.

So:

```bash
git add style.css
git commit --amend --no-edit
```

turns:

```text
Commit A
```

into an updated:

```text
Commit A'
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 16. Undo a Commit That Has Already Been Pushed

This is where things become more important.

Suppose:

```text
Local:
A → B → C

Remote:
A → B → C
```

You want to undo commit `C`.

If the commit has already been shared with others, the safest approach is usually:

```bash
git revert C
```

Git creates a new commit that reverses the changes introduced by `C`.

Result:

```text
A → B → C → D
             ↑
        revert C
```

Commit `D` effectively undoes `C`.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 17. `git revert` vs `git reset`

This distinction is extremely important.

| Situation                         | Recommended  |
| --------------------------------- | ------------ |
| Undo local commit                 | `git reset`  |
| Rewrite local history             | `git reset`  |
| Undo already-pushed/shared commit | `git revert` |
| Keep commit history unchanged     | `git revert` |

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Reset**

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

moves the branch backward.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Revert**

```bash
git revert HEAD
```

creates a new commit that reverses the previous commit.

For shared branches such as:

```text
main
production
```

`git revert` is generally safer than rewriting history.
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 18. Revert a Specific Older Commit

Suppose:

```text
A → B → C → D → E
```

You discover that `B` introduced a bug.

First find its hash:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

Example:

```text
e5a1234 Update documentation
d7b4321 Fix login
c8f1234 Update UI
b2a5678 Add authentication
a1c2345 Initial commit
```

Then:

```bash
git revert b2a5678
```

Git creates a new commit reversing the changes introduced by that commit.

The history becomes:

```text
A → B → C → D → E → F
                     ↑
               revert of B
```

The original commit remains in history.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 19. Undo Multiple Commits with Revert

You can revert multiple commits, but be careful about the order and whether the changes depend on each other.

For example:

```bash
git revert HEAD
```

undoes the latest commit.

You can also specify a range:

```bash
git revert OLDER_COMMIT..NEWER_COMMIT
```

Git will create revert commits for the specified commits.

If conflicts occur, resolve them and continue:

```bash
git add .
git revert --continue
```

To cancel the operation:

```bash
git revert --abort
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 20. Undo a Merge Commit

Merge commits require special handling.

Suppose:

```text
A → B → C
     \     \
      D → E → M
```

`M` is a merge commit.

To revert it:

```bash
git revert -m 1 <merge-commit>
```

The `-m 1` tells Git which parent should be treated as the mainline.

For example:

```bash
git revert -m 1 abc1234
```

This is particularly useful when a branch was merged into `main` and you want to undo the merge without rewriting shared history.
</merge-commit></md></sn><sn><md>#### 21. Cancel an In-Progress Merge

Suppose you run:

```bash
git merge production
```

and Git reports conflicts.

You decide:

&gt; I don't want this merge anymore.

Use:

```bash
git merge --abort
```

Git attempts to return your working tree to the state before the merge started.

Check:

```bash
git status
```

afterwards.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 22. Cancel an In-Progress Rebase

If you started:

```bash
git rebase main
```

and encounter problems, cancel it with:

```bash
git rebase --abort
```

This attempts to restore the branch to its previous state.

Other useful commands are:

```bash
git rebase --continue
```

and:

```bash
git rebase --skip
```

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 23. Cancel an In-Progress Cherry-Pick

If you run:

```bash
git cherry-pick abc1234
```

and get conflicts, you can cancel it:

```bash
git cherry-pick --abort
```

Or after resolving conflicts:

```bash
git add .
git cherry-pick --continue
```

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 24. Undo a `git add`

If you accidentally run:

```bash
git add .
```

and want to unstage everything:

```bash
git restore --staged .
```

Older Git syntax is:

```bash
git reset HEAD .
```

The modern `git restore` command is clearer because it specifically describes restoring the staging state.</md></sn><sn><md>#### 25. Undo a File Deletion

Suppose you deleted:

```text
index.php
```

but have not committed the deletion.

Run:

```bash
git restore index.php
```

Git restores the file from the latest commit.

If the deletion was staged:

```bash
git restore --staged index.php
```

first unstages the deletion.

Then:

```bash
git restore index.php
```

restores the file.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 26. Recover Something You Accidentally Deleted

Git often keeps old commits accessible even after you move the branch backward.

This is where:

```bash
git reflog
```

becomes extremely useful.

Run:

```bash
git reflog
```

You may see:

```text
abc1234 HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to HEAD~1
def5678 HEAD@{1}: commit: Important work
```

If you accidentally removed `def5678`, you can recover it.

For example:

```bash
git reset --hard def5678
```

or create a branch pointing to it:

```bash
git branch recovery def5678
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 27. What Is `git reflog`?

`git log` shows the commit history of a branch.

`git reflog` shows movements of references such as `HEAD`.

For example:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

might show:

```text
C
B
A
```

But after:

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

commit `C` may no longer appear in the branch's normal history.

However:

```bash
git reflog
```

can still show that `HEAD` previously pointed to `C`.

This makes `reflog` one of the most important recovery tools in Git.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 28. Undo a Push

There are two fundamentally different situations.

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Situation A**: The commit has been pushed but nobody else depends on it

You could rewrite history:

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
git push --force-with-lease
```

Prefer:

```bash
git push --force-with-lease
```

over:

```bash
git push --force
```

because `--force-with-lease` provides protection against overwriting remote work you have not seen.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Situation B**: Other people already pulled the commit

Do not normally rewrite shared history.

Instead:

```bash
git revert HEAD
git push
```

This preserves the existing history.
:::
:::

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 29. Undo a Force Push

A mistaken force push can sometimes be recovered using reflogs and remote-tracking information.

First inspect:

```bash
git reflog
```

Then identify the previous commit:

```text
abc1234
```

Create a recovery branch:

```bash
git branch recovery abc1234
```

Then inspect it before deciding how to restore the remote branch.

This is much safer than immediately running another force push.


</md></sn><sn><md>#### 30. Undo Changes from One Specific Commit

Suppose you have:

```text
A → B → C → D → E
```

and want to reverse only `C`.

Find its hash:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

Then:

```bash
git revert <commit-hash>
```

Example:

```bash
git revert c8f1234
```

This does **not** remove `C`.

Instead:

```text
A → B → C → D → E → F
                     ↑
                reverses C
```

This is usually the correct approach for shared repositories.
</commit-hash></md></sn><sn><md>#### 31. Undo Changes to One File from an Older Commit

Sometimes you don't want to revert an entire commit. You only want a particular file from an older commit.

You can restore a file from a specific commit:

```bash
git restore --source=<commit> -- path/to/file
```

Example:

```bash
git restore --source=abc1234 -- index.php
```

Now `index.php` contains the version from commit `abc1234`.

Then:

```bash
git add index.php
git commit -m "Restore index.php from previous version"
```

This is extremely useful when combining files from different versions or branches.
</commit></md></sn><sn><md>#### 32. Restore a File from Another Branch

Suppose you are currently on:

```text
main
```

and want the version of:

```text
config.php
```

from:

```text
production
```

You can use:

```bash
git restore --source=production -- config.php
```

Then check:

```bash
git diff
```

If it is correct:

```bash
git add config.php
git commit -m "Restore production config"
```

This changes only that file; it does not merge the entire branch.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 33. Undo a Branch Merge Before Committing

If Git is performing a merge and conflicts occur:

```bash
git merge --abort
```

If the merge has not created a commit yet, this is usually the cleanest way to cancel it.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 34. Undo a Completed Merge

If the merge has already been committed:

```text
A → B → M
```

where `M` is the merge commit, you generally have two options.


:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Shared branch**

Use:

```bash
git revert -m 1 <merge-commit>
```
:::

:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Private/local branch**

You can potentially reset:

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

The choice depends on whether the merge commit has already been shared.
:::
:::
</merge-commit></md></sn><sn><md>#### 35. Undo a Rebase

If the rebase is currently running:

```bash
git rebase --abort
```

If the rebase has already completed, `git reflog` can often help you find the branch position before the rebase.

For example:

```bash
git reflog
```

Find the entry before the rebase and then carefully restore it:

```bash
git reset --hard <old-commit>
```
</old-commit></md></sn><sn><md>#### 36. Undo a Cherry-Pick

If cherry-pick is in progress:

```bash
git cherry-pick --abort
```

If it has already created a commit, you can usually undo that commit with:

```bash
git revert <cherry-picked-commit>
```
</cherry-picked-commit></md></sn><sn><md>#### 37. Undo a Tag

If you accidentally created a local tag:

```bash
git tag v1.0
```

delete it with:

```bash
git tag -d v1.0
```

If the tag was pushed:

```bash
git push origin --delete v1.0
```

Be careful with deleting tags that others may already use.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 38. Undo a Branch Creation

A local branch can be deleted with:

```bash
git branch -d branch-name
```

If Git refuses because the branch contains unmerged commits:

```bash
git branch -D branch-name
```

`-D` forcefully deletes the branch reference.

The commits may still be recoverable through reflog for some time.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 39. Undo a Remote Branch Deletion

If you accidentally delete a remote branch:

```bash
git push origin --delete feature
```

you may be able to recreate it if you still know the commit:

```bash
git push origin <commit>:refs/heads/feature
```

Or, if a local branch still exists:

```bash
git push -u origin feature
```
</commit></md></sn><sn><md>#### 40. Undo File Changes Safely with Stash

Sometimes you don't actually want to delete your changes.

You just want to temporarily remove them.

Use:

```bash
git stash
```

Your working directory becomes clean, while your changes are stored in the stash.

Later:

```bash
git stash pop
```

restores them.

You can inspect stashes:

```bash
git stash list
```

Example:

```text
stash@{0}: WIP on main
stash@{1}: WIP on production
```

Restore a specific stash:

```bash
git stash apply stash@{0}
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 41. Delete a Stash

If you are sure you no longer need a stash:

```bash
git stash drop stash@{0}
```

Delete all stashes:

```bash
git stash clear
```

Be careful with `git stash clear`, because recovering cleared stashes can be difficult.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 42. A Complete Git Undo Decision Tree

When you want to undo something, first ask:

```text
What do I want to undo?
        │
        ├── Local file modification?
        │       └── git restore <file>
        │
        ├── Staged file?
        │       └── git restore --staged <file>
        │
        ├── Untracked file?
        │       └── rm / del / git clean
        │
        ├── Last local commit?
        │       ├── Keep staged → git reset --soft HEAD~1
        │       ├── Keep unstaged → git reset HEAD~1
        │       └── Delete changes → git reset --hard HEAD~1
        │
        ├── Already-pushed commit?
        │       └── git revert <commit>
        │
        ├── Merge currently running?
        │       └── git merge --abort
        │
        ├── Rebase currently running?
        │       └── git rebase --abort
        │
        ├── Cherry-pick currently running?
        │       └── git cherry-pick --abort
        │
        └── Lost commit?
                └── git reflog
```
</commit></file></file></md></sn><sn><md>#### 43. The Most Important Undo Commands

| Situation                           | Command                     |
| ----------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Discard file modification           | `git restore file`          |
| Discard all local modifications     | `git restore .`             |
| Unstage file                        | `git restore --staged file` |
| Unstage everything                  | `git restore --staged .`    |
| Delete untracked files              | `git clean -f`              |
| Preview untracked deletion          | `git clean -n`              |
| Undo last commit, keep staged       | `git reset --soft HEAD~1`   |
| Undo last commit, keep changes      | `git reset HEAD~1`          |
| Undo last commit and delete changes | `git reset --hard HEAD~1`   |
| Undo pushed commit safely           | `git revert HEAD`           |
| Revert specific commit              | `git revert <commit>`       |
| Cancel merge                        | `git merge --abort`         |
| Cancel rebase                       | `git rebase --abort`        |
| Cancel cherry-pick                  | `git cherry-pick --abort`   |
| Recover lost commits                | `git reflog`                |
| Temporarily save changes            | `git stash`                 |
| Restore stash                       | `git stash pop`             |
| Fix previous commit                 | `git commit --amend`        |
</commit></md></sn><sn><md>#### 44. `restore`, `reset`, `revert`, and `reflog` Compared

These four commands are often confused.

| Command       | Main purpose                                    |
| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| `git restore` | Restore files                                   |
| `git reset`   | Move branch/HEAD or change staging state        |
| `git revert`  | Create a new commit that reverses an old commit |
| `git reflog`  | Find previous positions of `HEAD`               |

A useful mental model is:

```text
restore → files
reset   → branch/history/staging
revert  → shared history
reflog  → recovery
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 45. The Golden Rule of Git Undo

Before using an undo command, determine:

```text
1. Is the change committed?
2. Is it staged?
3. Is it pushed?
4. Do I want to keep the changes?
5. Is the branch shared with other people?
```

Then choose the command.

For example:

- Modified file, not staged

```bash
git restore file
```

- Staged file

```bash
git restore --staged file
```

- Local commit, not pushed

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

or:

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

depending on whether you want to keep the changes.

- Pushed/shared commit

```bash
git revert <commit>
```

- Lost commit

```bash
git reflog
```
</commit></md></sn><sn><md>#### 46. Final Practical Example

Suppose your project has:

```text
main
production
```

You make changes to:

```text
index.php
config.php
style.css
```

Then:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Update project"
git push
```

Later you discover that `config.php` should not have been changed.

If the commit is already pushed, **do not reset the shared branch just to remove that file**.

Instead, restore the correct version of `config.php`:

```bash
git restore --source=HEAD~1 -- config.php
```

Then commit the correction:

```bash
git add config.php
git commit -m "Restore production config"
git push
```

History becomes:

```text
A → B → C → D
         │   │
         │   └── Restore config.php
         │
         └── Original update
```

This is often much safer for a shared `production` branch than rewriting its history.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 47. Conclusion

Git does not have one universal "undo" command. Different types of changes require different tools.

The essential commands to remember are:

```bash
git restore
git restore --staged
git reset
git revert
git reflog
git stash
```

The simplest way to remember them is:

```text
UNCOMMITTED FILE CHANGE
        ↓
    git restore

STAGED CHANGE
        ↓
git restore --staged

LOCAL COMMIT
        ↓
    git reset

SHARED/PUSHED COMMIT
        ↓
    git revert

LOST COMMIT / RECOVERY
        ↓
    git reflog

TEMPORARILY HIDE CHANGES
        ↓
    git stash
```

Most importantly, **be very careful with `git reset --hard`, `git clean -f`, and force-pushing**. These operations can permanently discard work or rewrite history. For shared branches, prefer creating a new corrective commit with `git revert`.
